  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nCold front is well south of the area now, with cooler air \nfiltering in. Satellite imagery showing high level clouds in \nplace across much of the area gradually pushing south as another\nlarge area of high pressure builds across SE Canada with \nridging extending into our area. A northerly flow will hold \ntemperatures below normal despite plenty of sunshine this \nafternoon. Highs expected to range from the upper 40s to mid \n50s. The high moves into our region tonight, but increasing \nclouds late should prevent temperatures from getting much \ncolder than guidance is indicating. It will still be chilly with\nlows in the 20s to lower 30s. An approaching warm front brings \nsome added cloud cover on Wed, with high expected to be close to\nnormal in the 50s to near 60F. Milder/above normal temperatures\nin store for Thu, as a SW flow results in an anomalously warm \nair mass (850 mb temperature anomalies of +1 to +2 STDEV). A \ncold front with limited moisture is expected to pass through \nThu night into Fri A.M. with just a few light showers in the \nAdirondacks. Temperatures should still be above normal on Fri in\nwake of the cold front due to deeper mixing developing \n(possibly up to ~800 mb). Another area of high pressure builds \nin over the weekend with continued dry weather, along with \nslightly above normal temperatures. Chances for showers may \nfinally arrive Sun night into Mon, although there is large model\nspread this far out so confidence is low.",
